WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.931
Telemetry forms the foundation of modern security operations.

2
00:00:03.931 --> 00:00:11.474
Visibility enables detection, investigation, response, threat hunting, reporting, and continuous improvement.

3
00:00:11.474 --> 00:00:22.061
Useful telemetry comes from many sources, including identities, endpoints, applications, networks, cloud services, infrastructure, and security tools.

4
00:00:22.061 --> 00:00:25.922
Collection should be driven by security questions and business priorities.

5
00:00:25.922 --> 00:00:36.389
Teams need to know which sources exist, who owns them, what activity they record, how long they retain data, and which gaps could prevent an important detection or investigation.

6
00:00:36.389 --> 00:00:38.544
Quality matters as much as volume.

7
00:00:38.544 --> 00:00:47.554
Completeness, accuracy, timeliness, normalization, enrichment, retention, and coverage determine whether analysts can rely on the data.

8
00:00:47.554 --> 00:00:53.630
Monitoring source health and testing expected events help keep visibility dependable as systems change.

9
00:00:53.630 --> 00:01:00.774
Organizations that understand and continuously refine their telemetry are better positioned to manage cybersecurity risk.

10
00:01:00.774 --> 00:01:03.069
The goal is not to collect everything.

11
00:01:03.069 --> 00:01:08.916
The goal is to maintain the evidence needed for informed defensive decisions when those decisions matter.

12
00:01:08.916 --> 00:01:20.810
A sustainable telemetry program assigns owners, documents purposes, protects sensitive data, monitors cost and source health, and reviews coverage when technology or risk changes.

13
00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:27.176
Managers, engineers, analysts, responders, privacy teams, and system owners all contribute.

14
00:01:27.176 --> 00:01:32.164
Their shared work turns disconnected event streams into dependable operational visibility.
